                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1785-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>Electro-Voice ND76</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Dynamic Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The EV ND76 is a stage vocal microphone. It is part of EV&#8217;s ND series of mics, and it replaces the N/D 767a mic, now discontinued. It is the least expensive vocal microphone in the ND line.</p><p>From specifications and available information, we conclude that all of the ND microphones (except from the small diaphragm condenser ND66) share a large diaphragm, moving-coil cartridge with a Mylar diaphragm. Exact measurements are unknown. </p><p>The capsule in the ND76, and all the ND dynamics, is shock mounted with a four-point suspension for durability and handling noise. It also contains a humbucking coil to combat electrical interference. The capsule is covered in a foam layer that protects against moisture. </p><p>The grill of the ND76 is made from EV&#8217;s &#8220;memraflex&#8221; material, which is a very rigid mesh that resists dents and damage from drops.</p><p>As the ND designation would indicate, the magnet in the ND76 is neodymium, which gives higher output and reduces physical weight.</p><p>The frequency chart for the ND76 shows a significant peak of +8db at 6kHz. This appears to be balanced by a significant proximity effect of +11db at 100Hz when the mic is placed 1cm from the source. </p><p>The ND76 is sold with a mic clip and cloth bag.</p><p>The ND76 is also available with an on/off switch as the ND76S (MSRP $198).<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Electro-Voice/ND76">Electro-Voice ND76</a> is also known as: ND 76, ND-76.</p><p>The mic was released in 2016.</p>
